<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lorenzo Valla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1407–<a href="/years/1457">1457</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lorenzo Valla was an Italian humanist, rhetorician and scholar. In 1440, while serving Alfonso V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, then at war for the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, he proved by linguistic and historical analysis that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donation-of-constantine">Donation of Constantine</a> was a forgery. His Elegantiae linguae Latinae became a standard guide to classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, and his critical notes on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a>. He later worked at the papal court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lorenzo-valla.jpg" alt="Lorenzo Valla" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portret_van_Lorenzo_Valla_Laurentius_Valla_(titel_op_object),_RP-P-1909-4359.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Valla was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> around 1407, of a family from Piacenza; his father, Luciave della Valla, was a lawyer in the Papal Curia. He attended the classes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni">Leonardo Bruni</a> and Giovanni Aurispa, from whom he learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek, and is thought otherwise to have been largely self-taught. He gave offence to Antonio Loschi by championing the rhetorician Quintilian in an early work. In 1431 he was ordained a priest and tried in vain to secure a post as apostolic secretary. He then went to Piacenza and Pavia, where he held a professorship of eloquence until his attack on the Latin style of the jurist Bartolus de Saxoferrato made his stay uncomfortable, after which he lectured in many cities.</p><p>From 1435 to 1445 Valla was employed at the court of Alfonso V of Aragon, who was in a territorial conflict with the Papal States under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-eugene-iv">Pope Eugene IV</a>. In 1439–1440 he wrote his essay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donation-of-constantine">Donation of Constantine</a>, which claimed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> to the Church in gratitude for a cure from leprosy by Pope Sylvester I. In 1444 he was put on trial before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> but was protected from imprisonment by Alfonso. Pope Nicholas V invited him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1447, where he worked on his Repastinatio, became a papal scribe and in 1455 a papal secretary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Valla argued that the Donation's internal evidence told against a 4th-century origin, since its vernacular style could be dated to the 8th century. He held that Constantine could not legally have given Sylvester such powers, that the absence of contemporary evidence made so great a change implausible, and that the text used the word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> would not have used, and switched tenses in poor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. In textual criticism he proposed emendations to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>. In his study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a>'s Vulgate he questioned the system of penance and indulgences, arguing that paenitentia rendered the Greek metanoia, better translated as repentance, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> praised the work.</p><p>His dialogue De Voluptate and his treatise on the elegances of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> made his reputation in his lifetime. Richard Claverhouse Jebb said the latter marked the highest level yet reached in the critical study of Latin, and it was reprinted nearly sixty times between 1471 and 1536.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Valla died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 1 August 1457 and was buried beneath the bronze Lex de imperio Vespasiani behind the altar of Saint John Lateran. In 1576 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xiii">Pope Gregory XIII</a>, a staunch believer in the Donation, had his tomb and epitaph destroyed when he moved the bronze; a memorial to Valla stands in the Lateran today. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> thought highly of him, Robert Bellarmine called him Luther's precursor, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> wrote that for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> grammar there was no better guide.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lorenzo Valla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alfonso V made Naples a centre of Renaissance humanism, patronising scholars such as <b>Lorenzo Valla</b> and Antonio Beccadelli, whose circle grew into the academy later named after Giovanni Pontano, and the triumphal arch of the Castel Nuovo is among the earliest Renaissance monuments of southern Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Around this time the forged Donation of Constantine, claiming that the first Christian emperor had granted the pope authority over the West, was produced; <b>Lorenzo Valla</b> proved it a forgery in 1440.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1440 <b>Lorenzo Valla</b>, in the service of Alfonso V of Aragon, then at war with the pope over the Kingdom of Naples, used the history of Latin words and institutions to show that the Donation of Constantine, long cited in support of the popes’ worldly power, could…</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1439" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lorenzo Valla"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1439">What was here in 1439? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I know that for a long time now men’s ears are waiting to hear the offense with which I charge the Roman pontiffs.&#8221;</p><footer>On the Forged Donation of Constantine (1440) &#183; tr. Christopher B. Coleman, 1922 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/70092/pg70092-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;With a brave soul, with utter fidelity, with good hope, the cause of truth must be defended, the cause of justice, the cause of God.&#8221;</p><footer>On the Forged Donation of Constantine (1440) &#183; tr. Christopher B. Coleman, 1922 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/70092/pg70092-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But I want to take the forger himself, truly a “straw” man without wheat, by the neck, and drag him into court.&#8221;</p><footer>On the Forged Donation of Constantine (1440) &#183; tr. Christopher B. Coleman, 1922 &#183; of the forger of the Donation of Constantine &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/70092/pg70092-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
